Z={
1:"Commentators note paramaṃ vacaḥ, “supreme word”: Kṛṣṇa continues because Arjuna listens with joy. Madhusūdana says the Lord’s motive is Arjuna’s good. Many teachers say a student’s delight is the best encouragement a teacher can receive.",
2:"Commentators note sarvaśaḥ, “in every way”: the Lord is the source of gods and sages in every respect. Madhusūdana says a source is always prior to and greater than what comes from it. The Kena Upaniṣad tells how even the gods did not recognise Brahman when it appeared before them as a mysterious being.",
3:"Commentators note martyeṣu, “among mortals”: such knowledge is rare among humans. Madhusūdana says it removes the delusion that we are self-made. The verse invites gratitude toward the source of all.",
4:"Commentators note that bhava and abhāva, birth and death, are both from the Lord. Madhusūdana says even fear has its purpose: it protects us from danger. Seeing every state as coming from one source makes it easier to bear the hard ones.",
5:"Commentators note pṛthag-vidhāḥ, “of various kinds”: the states are many, the source one. Madhusūdana says each arises according to karma. The list of virtues here has been used in Indian schools as a guide to character.",
6:"Commentators note mad-bhāvāḥ, “filled with my nature”: the ancestors of humanity carry the Lord’s nature. Madhusūdana says they were born of the Lord’s mind, not by ordinary birth. Many Hindu families trace their lineage, gotra, to one of the seven sages.",
7:"Commentators note avikampena, “unshaking”: knowing the Lord’s glory makes devotion steady. Madhusūdana says wavering comes from seeing the world apart from God. Seeing God in the world removes the cause of wavering.",
8:"Commentators note budhāḥ, “the wise”: wisdom and love go together. Madhusūdana says knowing the Lord as the origin of all naturally leads to worship. The verse dissolves the supposed conflict between thinking and feeling.",
9:"Commentators note mad-gata-prāṇāḥ, “whose lives are given to me”: devotees live for the Lord. Madhusūdana says their talk is always of him. Bhajan and satsaṅg groups meet in homes and temples across India to sing and speak of the Lord, as this verse describes.",
10:"Commentators note satata-yuktānām, “of the ever-united”: steady devotion receives understanding. Madhusūdana says the Lord gives the right understanding at the right time. Many devotees report that insight came not from study alone but from love.",
11:"Commentators note bhāsvatā, “shining”: the lamp is bright. Madhusūdana says the Lord within dispels the darkness of ignorance. The festival of Dīpāvalī, the row of lamps, celebrates the victory of light over darkness, inner as well as outer.",
12:"Commentators note puruṣaṃ śāśvatam, “eternal Person”: Arjuna recognises the eternal in his friend. Madhusūdana says this praise shows his understanding has deepened. The titles he uses come from the Upaniṣads.",
13:"Commentators note that the sages named were known in the epic for their wisdom. Madhusūdana says their testimony confirms Kṛṣṇa’s words. Asita Devala is also remembered as an authority on dharma, quoted in later law-books.",
14:"Commentators note na dānavāḥ, “nor the demons”: neither gods nor demons know the Lord’s full nature. Madhusūdana says this is why the Lord must reveal himself. The verse prepares Arjuna’s request.",
15:"Commentators note jagat-pate, “Lord of the world”: Arjuna’s titles rise in a crescendo. Madhusūdana says the repetition expresses wonder. Hymns of praise in many traditions build in this way, name upon name.",
16:"Commentators note vyāpya tiṣṭhasi, “you pervade and abide”: Arjuna wants to know how the Lord fills the world. Madhusūdana says the request is for meditation. Seeing the Divine in particular forms makes the infinite approachable.",
17:"Commentators note paricintayan, “meditating”: Arjuna wants to remember the Lord constantly. Madhusūdana says the glories will serve as reminders. Many people keep a small image or symbol at their workplace for the same reason.",
18:"Commentators note tṛptiḥ, “satisfaction”: Arjuna is never satisfied. Madhusūdana says this hunger is a sign of true love. Lovers never tire of hearing about the beloved.",
19:"Commentators note divyāḥ, “divine”: the glories are not ordinary. Madhusūdana says only the chief ones can be named. The list that follows mixes the cosmic, the natural and the human.",
20:"Commentators note āśaya, “heart” or “resting place”: the Self dwells in every heart. Ramana Maharshi often quoted this verse, saying the Self in the heart is the one reality behind all the names in the list.",
21:"Commentators note aṃśumān, “radiant”: the sun is the brightest of lights. Madhusūdana says the Lord is the excellence in each group. Some read the moon among the stars as the Lord’s gentleness, the sun as his power.",
22:"Commentators note that consciousness, cetanā, is the spark that makes beings alive. Madhusūdana says the Lord is that spark. Modern science has no full account of consciousness; the Gītā places it at the heart of the Divine.",
23:"Commentators note vitteśa, “lord of wealth”: Kubera guards the treasures of the earth. Madhusūdana says the Lord is the best among the yakṣas. Meru, the golden mountain, is pictured in temple architecture: many temple towers are said to represent it.",
24:"Commentators note mukhyam, “chief”: Bṛhaspati is the chief priest. Murugan temples in Tamil Nadu, such as Palani and Tiruchendur, draw millions of pilgrims each year.",
25:"Commentators note sthāvarāṇām, “of the immovable”: the Himālaya is the greatest of mountains. Madhusūdana says japa is the best sacrifice because it harms no living being. Many pilgrims walk to Himalayan shrines like Kedārnāth and Badrīnāth.",
26:"Commentators note devarṣi, “divine sage”: Nārada moves between gods and humans. Madhusūdana says Kapila is the best of the perfected for his knowledge. Nārada’s Bhakti Sūtras, a short text on devotion, are widely studied.",
27:"Commentators note amṛtodbhava, “born of nectar”: the horse rose from the churning of the ocean. Madhusūdana says the just king is the Lord’s glory among people. The verse honours good governance as sacred.",
28:"Commentators note āyudhānām, “of weapons”: the thunderbolt is the mightiest. Madhusūdana says Kāmadhenu, the wish-fulfilling cow, gives all that is needed. Cows are honoured in India partly for this reason, as givers of milk and life.",
29:"Commentators note varuṇa, lord of waters: in the Vedas he also watches over truth and order. Madhusūdana says Yama is the best of those who restrain because none escapes death. The verse includes both the gentle and the stern.",
30:"Commentators note kalayatām, “of reckoners”: time measures all. Madhusūdana says the lion is king of beasts and Garuḍa king of birds. Garuḍa is honoured in Vaiṣṇava temples, often facing the main shrine.",
31:"Commentators note pavatām, “of purifiers”: the wind purifies. Madhusūdana says Rāma is the best of warriors. The river Gaṅgā is believed to purify all who bathe in her with faith; the Kumbh Melā at Prayāg draws millions to her banks.",
32:"Commentators note vidyānām, “of sciences”: the science of the Self is the highest. Madhusūdana says honest discussion, vāda, leads to truth. The Upaniṣads themselves are largely made of such discussions between teachers and students.",
33:"Commentators note akṣayaḥ kālaḥ, “imperishable time”: time without end. Madhusūdana says the Lord is time itself, the dispenser of all fruits. The verse includes language and grammar among the Lord’s glories, a rare honour for scholarship.",
34:"Commentators note sarva-haraḥ, “all-taking”: death takes all. Madhusūdana says the Lord is also the origin of all that will be. The seven qualities listed are often praised as ideals of character.",
35:"Madhusūdana says spring is the best season because it is full of flowers. Many poets, from Kālidāsa onward, celebrated spring as the season of joy.",
36:"Commentators note vyavasāyaḥ, “effort” or “determination”: the Lord is in honest striving. Madhusūdana says the Lord is also victory for the victorious. The verse ends with goodness, sattva, as the Lord’s presence in the good.",
37:"Commentators note that the verse names Kṛṣṇa’s own clan, his friend, the epic’s author and a great teacher. Madhusūdana says the Lord shines in the best of each group. Arjuna, hearing his own name, is honoured.",
38:"Commentators note nītiḥ, “policy” or “strategy”: wise strategy is divine. Madhusūdana says the Lord is silence among secrets. Many traditions value silence as the language of the deepest truth.",
39:"Commentators note carācaram, “moving and unmoving”: nothing exists without the Lord. Madhusūdana says the Lord is the seed of all. The verse turns the list from the extraordinary to the ordinary.",
40:"Commentators note uddeśataḥ, “by way of example”: the list is a sample. Madhusūdana says the Lord’s glories are infinite. The verse invites each reader to continue the list from their own experience.",
41:"Commentators note tejo’ṃśa-sambhavam, “born of a spark of my splendour”: all excellence is a spark of the Lord. Madhusūdana says seeing this removes pride in one’s own gifts. Great artists in India traditionally began their work with a prayer, offering their talent to its source.",
42:"Commentators note viṣṭabhya, “supporting”: the Lord holds up the universe with a fraction of himself. Madhusūdana says this prepares for the cosmic vision of chapter 11. The chapter ends with a hint of what Arjuna will soon see.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
